WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.860
We've been talking a lot about Bernie Sanders
lately.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:08.590
We've been talking a lot about Joe Biden and
how he fits into the 2020 democratic primary.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:13.599
We talked yesterday on the bonus show about
how Beto O'Rourke has almost totally disappeared

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:18.529
from media coverage and his polling is down
as well from the eight to 12 range.

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:25.060
Beto is down to as low as 3% in two recent
polls, but what about Elizabeth Warren?

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.839
Warren is doing some really good stuff.

00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:31.509
Warren is not only starting to get some headway
and positive feedback in Makkah country at

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:33.480
the events that she's been holding.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.250
She's also pushed into the double digits in
a lot of recent polls and she's not doing

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:42.950
it with flash or charisma because as I've
said before, I don't find her particularly

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:48.360
rife with charisma or relate-ability, but
she's doing it with policy proposals that

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.970
are really interesting, many of which makes
sense.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:56.260
Now, first of all, lots of right wingers have
been skeptical of Warren, some kind of anti

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.940
business, anti entrepreneurship type A and
she's really not.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.320
So when she shows up and talks about letting
entrepreneurs and markets work in a controlled

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.609
way, that actually is having some appeal across
party lines, but she also has some really

00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:16.829
specific and solid proposals that are strongly
socially democratic.

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:20.299
And this is a lot of the stuff I've been talking
about for years.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:24.320
So let's look at some examples of Elizabeth
Warren's really great policy proposals.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.329
Number one, antitrust.

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:31.069
Elizabeth Warren wants to pass legislation
that will require large tech platforms be

00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:39.560
designated as platform utilities and get broken
apart or separated from a participants on

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.039
that platform.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:47.119
She also, as part of her antitrust ideas,
would appoint regulators committed to reversing

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.499
illegal and anti competitive tech mergers,
really important.

00:01:51.499 --> 00:01:55.100
And she talks about protecting the future
of the Internet, which is of course the importance

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.759
of net neutrality, really great stuff.

00:01:57.759 --> 00:01:59.390
And I'm completely behind it.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.280
She's also proposed an ultra millionaire tax,
and this would be a wealth tax on the richest

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:09.810
Americans and it would apply to households
with a net worth of over $50 million.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:15.240
So this is only about 75 households, uh, in
a country of 340 million people, it's the

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.650
top 0.1%.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:23.850
The way it would work is you pay a 2% tax
on every dollar of net worth, above $50 million

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:28.190
and a 3% tax on every dollar of net worth
above a billion.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:32.620
Uh, this is expected to raise nearly $3 trillion
over a 10 year period.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.450
Now, I do have a concern about this.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:41.480
I'm unsure about this proposal, but it's a
specific proposal and I think it is fantastic

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.080
that Elizabeth Warren is talking really specifically.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.650
My concern about the wealth tax is that a
lot of that net worth that people have on

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.230
paper is not liquid.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:56.340
And I don't think it would be a good idea
to enforce attacks that might require rich

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.220
people to sell assets in order to pay the
wealth tax.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.720
And, um, that's just kind of like a question
mark for me.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.960
Uh, I also have other questions about whether
we should be focused on wealth or maybe looking

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.770
at capital gains more, more importantly, but
it doesn't matter, right?

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.540
Like my exact opinion on this idea doesn't
matter.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.610
This is a very specific and ambitious proposal.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.640
Um, universal childcare, the Universal Childcare
proposal that Elizabeth Warren has put out

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:30.540
there would see the government partner with
local childcare providers and childcare would

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.220
be free to anyone who is at 200% of the federal
poverty line or below.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:43.150
Uh, and then if you are above 200% of the
federal poverty line, the cost of childcare

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:46.600
would be capped at 7% of income.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.790
It would be paid for by the ultra millionaire
attacks that I just told you about.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.650
Very interesting and important and there are
positive externalities of freeing up people

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:02.500
to go and work and earn wages that are good
for the government and they're good for those

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:03.500
families.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.319
And it's a really interesting proposal.

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.990
Elizabeth Warren has a housing investment
plan, which she says we'll bring down rental

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:15.680
costs by 10% over 10 years and create a million
and a half jobs in construction and renovation.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.810
It would see the government invest 500 billion
over the next 10 years to build, preserve,

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:26.430
and preserve and renovate units of housing
units, which will be affordable to low income

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.630
families.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:33.990
This is textbook social democracy and she
would pay for it by a tweak to the estate

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.040
tax.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.350
This is another very good proposal from Elizabeth
Warren.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:45.580
You may remember that when George W. Bush
was in office, the first 7 million of an estate

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.860
were exempt from taxes.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.210
That has been raised it to $22 million.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:05:00.270
Elizabeth Warren would simply lower it back
to $7 million and uh, also, uh, tweak some

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.190
of the numbers in terms of the estate tax
rates for people who have really, really big

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.370
estates.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:11.020
I can go on, there's many more of these proposals,
but this is a sampling and what is really

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:16.710
important to understand about this is that
Elizabeth Warren is not changing the capitalist

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.340
system that we have.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.710
She's allowing entrepreneurship, she's allowing
business, she's allowing people to get very

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.820
rich and all of that stuff.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:30.390
But she's saying we're going to use some of
that money to keep people from being left

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.390
behind.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:36.990
We're going to have relatively modest government
involvement, a little more in line with some

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.780
of those Nordic and Scandinavian economies.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.850
And these are serious specific proposals that
go way beyond what many other candidates are

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.170
doing or talking about.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.160
So this is a substance over style approach.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.160
It may not work, right?

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.370
I mean she's polling third or fourth depending
on the poll that you look at.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:59.590
At a certain point, if she's going to be the
nominee, she needs to start making more headway

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:01.080
than she has.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.810
And I don't know that this substance over
style approach will actually appeal to enough

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.830
people.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:12.410
Biden's forward polling 40% uh, and he's so
far has been running almost exclusively on

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:17.300
Trump is bad and I will defeat him, which
I've pointed out is not a great way to run

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.300
a campaign.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:24.220
Bernie, on the other hand is a more uh, in
line with the progressive ideas that people

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:28.090
are actually looking for when they are pulled
fairly about what they think government should

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.090
be doing.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.990
And he's polling 14 to 18% depending on the
pole.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.710
And Warren is putting out detailed policy
proposals that make a lot of sense and she's

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.970
pulling like seven to 12.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.260
So I don't know that substance over style
is going to get you the nomination.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:48.190
I don't know that if it gets through the nomination
that it will get you the presidency.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:53.270
Very depressing unfortunately, but really
great that Elizabeth Warren is doing this.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.490
And it is appealing to some voters who are
taking the

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:01.270
time to go to her town hall events and listen
to what she has to say.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:05.460
Let me know what you think about her proposals
about this approach of the show is on Instagram

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:05.480
at David Tactic

